  "query_preview": "..(This Afternoon Through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1255 PM MDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nThunderstorms are trying to develop over the central and southern\nBlack Hills as of the early afternoon hours. Destabilization will\ncontinue as surface temps warm to the 80s to low 90s. While most \nof the expected storms will be below severe thresholds, we can't \nrule out a few severe storms near the Black Hills and in southwest\nand south central SD. Main threats would be hail and wind.\n\nFor Friday and the weekend, afternoon temps will be warmer with \nhighs in the upper 80s to mid 90s Friday and Saturday, and only a \nfew degrees cooler on Sunday. Overall, well above normal for late\nAugust.\n\nThe upper level pattern looks to change a little bit as well. The\nridge of high pressure over the western US looks to slide east.\nThis would give the area west to southwest flow, opening the door\nfor daily showers and thunderstorms. As of now, the thinking is\nthat any storm development would start in central Wyoming, and \nnear the Black Hills, and those storms would then track eastward.\nSPC and machine learning severe weather models are highlighting\nsome risk of severe weather, but it's marginal (15% chance or \nless) for now. So we can't rule severe storms out but it's not a \nclear signal for widespread severe weather.\n\nLingering low end chances for daily showers and storms last well\ninto next week but the overall pattern is still on the dry side.\nAbove normal temps will continue as well with highs in the 80s to\n90s.",
